The '''History of Sarta (1811–1846)''' covers the period from the end of the [[Second Sartan Civil War]] to the return of the coastal territories in the [[Occidental Agreement of 1846]]. The era was marked by political caution, administrative centralisation, and a widening debate over whether Sarta should continue to live under Volisanian pressure or pursue a more independent course. Although the crown remained in place, domestic politics increasingly shifted toward parliamentary and bureaucratic rule.

== Postwar settlement ==

After 1811, support grew in Sarta for recovering the coastal territories held by [[Volisania]]. The question was driven by domestic pressure, but it was also shaped by diplomacy. [[Jacan]] and [[Echia]] both pressed for a more stable balance in Illypnia, fearing that renewed division in the region would only strengthen [[Fosian]] powers. In Sarta, that argument gave additional weight to calls for a settlement that would restore lost land without triggering another civil war.

The political class remained cautious. Many lords remembered the destruction of the earlier civil wars and were unwilling to push the kingdom into open conflict. As a result, the first decades of the period were shaped less by confrontation than by negotiation, bureaucratic consolidation, and the slow formation of rival political alignments.

== The Constitution of 1812 ==

The most important domestic change of the period was the [[Constitution of 1812]], which standardised the kingdom's legal and administrative framework. It formalised a system of municipalities and provinces, regularised tax codes, and expanded the bureaucratic machinery of the state. The reform gave Sarta a more uniform internal structure and made taxation, local administration, and provincial oversight more predictable.

The constitution also limited the king's powers and placed them under a unicameral parliament that remained controlled by lords. The monarchy was not abolished, but it became more constrained. The result was a state that was more orderly and more legible than before, while still remaining firmly oligarchic. The political settlement was designed to prevent another dynastic crisis as much as to broaden participation.

== Political alignments ==

Two broad political consensuses emerged from the new order. One accepted continued cooperation with Volisania and sought to work within the limits created by the postwar settlement. The other wanted greater freedom from Volisanian political influence and argued for a stronger Sartan position. These alignments were not formal mass parties, but they shaped parliamentary debate, elite alliances, and regional politics for the rest of the period.

The pro-Volisania bloc remained influential among lords and commercial interests that benefited from stability and access to trade. The freer-from-Volisania current was strongest among lords south of the [[Sierra Madre]] and had its main stronghold in [[Leona]]. Even there, however, most nobles remained cautious. Fear of another civil war kept the political order from breaking into open conflict, and most disputes were managed through negotiation rather than direct confrontation.

== Urban migration and peasant unrest ==

As administration and mining expanded, population movement toward the towns increased. Many peasants left inland estates for urban labour, service work, and mining support, while rural manors continued to face periodic rebellions in their serf populations. The most serious of these was the [[Sartan Peasants Revolution of 1837]], in which roughly 10,000 serfs rose up in and around [[Leona]] against abuse in power and rural poverty. The revolt was suppressed, but it exposed the weakness of the kingdom's existing internal security system and pushed the lords' parliament to replace estate levies with a formal national force.

The pressure was eased but not removed by the [[Act of Standing Military of 1837]] and the [[Act on Free Movement of 1838]]. The first created a standing national military instead of depending on individual lordships to lend their own forces; the second abolished serfdom and compensated the lords. Together, the measures emptied the treasury heavily, leaving the crown's finances weak even as the cities and mines grew.

The king remained a figurehead of stability with considerable personal sway, but domestic affairs were increasingly handled by the lords' parliament and the ministerial bureaucracy.

== Coal and inland growth ==

In 1844, the first major coal mines were established in Sarta. The richest deposits lay in the [[Sierra Madre]], and the new mines began exporting primarily to Volisania, which had a strong demand for coal. The development brought rapid growth to the northern half of Sarta, where mining, transport, and related trade expanded quickly around the new extraction zones.

Coal wealth did not transform the whole kingdom evenly, but it did accelerate the development of the northern interior. Roads, towns, and service networks grew around the mines, while the political importance of the Sierra Madre increased. The new wealth also made the question of sovereignty more complicated, since the kingdom's economy now depended in part on trade with the same power that controlled the coast.

== The Occidental Agreement of 1846 ==

In 1846, the [[Occidental Agreement of 1846]] returned the coastal territories to Sarta. The restored lands were reorganised as [[Occidental Province]], and the agreement was negotiated in exchange for a 50% discount on coal exports to Volisania for the next 99 years. The settlement gave Sarta back its coast, but it also tied the kingdom more tightly to Volisanian economic needs.

Anti-Volisanian critics called the agreement the [[Tontas Trato]] or "foolish deal". They argued that international pressure would eventually force the return of the territories without requiring such a long and costly concession. Supporters of the agreement treated it as a practical compromise that recovered land while preserving peace and commercial continuity. In either case, the arrangement ended the long postwar question of the coast and reshaped the kingdom's territorial map.

== Aftermath ==

By 1846, Sarta had become a more centralised, bureaucratic, and politically cautious kingdom than it had been in 1811. The constitution of 1812 had narrowed royal power, the coal boom had strengthened the northern interior, and the Occidental Agreement of 1846 had restored the coast at a considerable economic cost. The kingdom remained divided between those who accepted accommodation with Volisania and those who saw the treaty as a surrender of leverage.

The period set the stage for later arguments over sovereignty, trade, and political reform. It also left Sarta with a more uniform state structure and a deeper dependence on export coal, even as it regained the territories that had defined its earlier losses.

